-- Algebraic hierarchy: how `Declares` extends supertypes with `via`, how
-- `specifies:` types tuple components and multi-view slices, and how
-- `satisfies:` records the algebraic axioms.


-- A magma pairs a carrier set `X` with a binary operation `*`. `is \set via X`
-- regards the magma as a set through `X` and types `X` as `\set`, so
-- `specifies:` only has to type `*`.
[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \operation:on{X}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x_ "in" X
Appendix:
. called: "magma"
Id: "0e5be05c-2b6d-48cc-8dec-d656eb6bb8b7"


-- A monoid extends a magma with an associative operation and a two-sided
-- identity element `e`. `via (X, *)` types both `X` and `*` positionally.
[\monoid]
Declares: M ::= (X, *, e) is \magma via (X, *)
specifies: e "in" X
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b, c "in" X
  then: (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
. forAll: a "in" X
  then:
  . e * a = a
  . a * e = a
Appendix:
. called: "monoid"
Id: "b41b952a-239a-49de-b27a-a896f0494b30"


-- A group is a monoid in which every element has a two-sided inverse.
-- Because `via (X, *, e)` covers every tuple component, no `specifies:` is needed.
[\group]
Declares: G ::= (X, *, e) is \monoid via (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: a "in" X
  then:
  . exists: b "in" X
    suchThat:
    . a * b = e
    . b * a = e
Appendix:
. called: "group"
Id: "bc7fdaea-a45a-4569-8607-78555d8a03eb"


-- A refinement preserves its base type's component types and adds commutativity.
[\(abelian)::group]
Refines: G ::= (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b "in" X
  then: a * b = b * a
Appendix:
. adjective: "abelian"
Id: "9ed8036e-561f-4fb9-80bf-2bd214428bc7"


-- Subtype views may live in `specifies:` when different slices of one tuple
-- implement different structures. Both views specify the shared carrier `X` as
-- `\set`, and `satisfies:` adds the left and right distributive laws.
[\ring]
Declares: R ::= (X, +_R, *_R, 0, 1)
specifies:
. R is \(abelian)::group via (X, +_R, 0)
. R is \monoid via (X, *_R, 1)
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b, c "in" X
  then:
  . (a *_R (b +_R c)) = ((a *_R b) +_R (a *_R c))
  . ((a +_R b) *_R c) = ((a *_R c) +_R (b *_R c))
Appendix:
. called: "ring"
Id: "a73b420d-f146-4142-bf5d-270916506a47"


-- `extends:` is the section form of extension, used when a structure extends
-- several types at once through different views of the same tuple.
[\bimagma]
Declares: B ::= (X, *, +)
extends:
. B is \magma via (X, *)
. B is \magma via (X, +)
Appendix:
. called: "bimagma"
Id: "fde4a779-17a4-48a3-997b-ad20380eb324"


-- Subtyping is recursive, so every group is a monoid, a magma, and a set, and
-- every element's inverse is unique.
[\group.identity.unique]
Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. e' "in" X
where:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: x * e' = x
then:
. G is? \monoid
. G is? \magma
. G is? \set
. X is? \set
. e "in"? X
. e' = e
Proof: "Since {. e "in"? X .}, the right-identity property of {. e' .} gives
        {. e * e' = e .}, while the left-identity property of {. e .} gives
        {. e * e' = e' .}. Therefore {. e' = e .}."
Appendix:
. called: "uniqueness of the identity in a group"
Id: "da7562a6-f7e7-4e0e-ba84-30ed132faeb1"


-- Both subtype slices of a ring contribute facts and component types.
Theorem:
given: R ::= (X, +_R, *_R, 0, 1) is \ring
then:
. R is? \(abelian)::group
. R is? \monoid
. X is? \set
. 0 "in"? X
. 1 "in"? X
. forAll: a, b "in" X
  then: (a +_R b) = (b +_R a)
Appendix:
. called: "a ring inherits both its additive group and multiplicative monoid"
Id: "60f94c0e-e5c7-40cb-b082-f2ab7bb0f6db"
